3. Details: Strategy Across the Ten Rounds
High-Level Evolution

Rounds 1–3: Exploration Phase

Broad sampling of the search space.

Construction of initial surrogate models.

Understanding function structure (smoothness, noise, sparsity).

Rounds 4–6: Transition Phase

Increased exploitation.

Reduced exploration parameters (e.g., β in UCB).

Introduction of progress validation checks.

Noise-aware modelling for noisy functions.

Gradient-based optimisation for higher-dimensional functions.

Rounds 7–10: Refinement Phase

Focused search near best-known points.

Switched from purely exploitative UCB to Expected Improvement (EI) when stagnation occurred.

Increased restarts for local optimisation.

Fine-tuning neural network surrogates.

Techniques Used by Function Type
Function Type	Dimensions	Method Used	Key Components
Low-dimensional smooth	2D–5D	Gaussian Process BO	RBF kernel, UCB and EI acquisition
Noisy	3D	Noise-aware GP	RBF + WhiteKernel
Higher-dimensional	6D–8D	Neural Network + Gradient	PyTorch MLP, multi-start gradient ascent
Structured/sparse	2D	Non-linear Logistic Regression	Polynomial features, probability-based sampling
Decision-Making Logic

The strategy chooses the next point by:

Fitting a surrogate model to observed data.

Using an acquisition function (UCB or EI) or gradient optimisation.

Evaluating progress against the incumbent best.

Adjusting exploration level if improvement stalls.

Strength: Flexible and adaptive.
Limitation: Relies heavily on surrogate accuracy.

4. Performance
Evaluation Setup

Eight black-box functions.

One submission per function per week.

Primary goal: maximise output value.

Metrics Used

Primary metric: Maximum observed function value.

Secondary metrics:

Week-on-week improvement.

Distance to incumbent best.

Acquisition value diagnostics.

There was no formal train/test split; evaluation occurred via live submissions.

Overall Results

Strong performance on smooth low-dimensional functions using GP-based BO.

Improved stability on noisy functions using noise-aware modelling.

Neural network surrogates showed diminishing returns in 6D–8D due to limited data.

Performance generally improved over rounds, though some plateauing occurred near optima.

